## INTRODUCTION
The nodes of some metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are essentially small,uniform metal oxide clusters, exemplified by the $Z r_{6}$ nodes of UiO-66,1 UiO-67, and NU-1oo0.2 The strong coordination bonds between these nodes and carboxylate oxygen atoms in the linkers help to stabilize these MOFs, which have been used at high temperatures (in the range of $300{-}400~\mathsf{9}0~\mathsf{C}^{3})$ and are therefore attractive in prospect for application as catalysts and catalyst supports. The crystal structures of these MOFs have been determined by X-ray diffraction crystallography, but this technique is not sufficient to determine the identities and placement of small non-structural ligands (i.e., ligands other than linkers) such as hydroxyl and aqua groups on the nodes.
The nonstructural ligands on the nodes of UiO66 and UiO-67 are associated with missing linkers (and, in some cases, nodes6-8). The nodes of UiOtype MOFs are nominally (or ideally) twelve-linkerconnected, but in real samples the number is less. The hexa-metal(IV) nodes of NU-1oo0, in contrast, are inherently eight- rather than twelveconnected, thus leaving numerous sites for binding of nonstructural ligands, even in the absence of structural defects.5 The node-ligated aqua and hydroxo species are Bronsted acids comparable to those on the surfaces of metal oxides and zeolites, and the corresponding MOFs are therefore comparable to these materials as catalysts and catalyst supports, albeit with potentially different properties owing to the nanoscale of the nodes and/or local confinement by the framework.
Because MOFs in prospect have structures that can be tuned more precisely than those of metal oxides and even zeolites, they offer excellent opportunities to create unique, uniform surface sites that exhibit new, controllable reactivities and catalytic properties—and, correspondingly, they offer additional opportunities for the anchoring of unique catalytic sites such as metal complexes. Therefore, there is a strong incentive for investigating the precise molecular structures of MOF nodes and their vacancies,and for tailoring them when possible for specific functions, a subject that is still at an early stage of investigation. An important recent contribution in this respect is the work of Ling and Slater,14 who investigated the dynamic acidity and interconversion of alternative defect terminations of UiO-66 in aqueous solution, extending the understanding15 with respect to the proton topology of such node faces in water.
Using a combination of infrared (lR) spectroscopy and density functional theory (DFT) calculations,16 we previously inferred that the NU1000 nodes and their proton topology are well represented as $[Z\Gamma_{6}(\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{4}(\mathsf{O}\bar{\mathsf{H}})_{4}(\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}_{2})_{4}]^{8+}$ with each structural vacancy occupied by a combination of terminal OH and $O H_{2}$ groups at sites referred to as site 1 (Scheme 1). This same terminating functionality was also identified at the missing linker sites of UiO-66 and UiO-67 nodes.4 Moreover, a second topology was observed for the nodes of UiO-66 and designated in the earlier work as site 2. The topology for site 2 was originally assigned as a node face having a pair of terminal OH groups, as illustrated in Scheme 1. The chemistries of these two sites were investigated in experiments with iridium carbonyl complexes as probes, and the chemisorbed iridium carbonyls were characterized by extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) and IR spectra, with the latter demonstrating different electron-donor properties of the two distinct sites.
An open challenge is to tune those groups terminating defects or vacancies on MOF node faces. Methods for interconverting such groups on individual faces are lacking and would be of particular value to the extent that they might modulate the properties of nodes and their reactivities for applications in catalysis and adsorption.
Scheme 1. Simplified depictions of the $z_{\Gamma_{6}}$ nodeface topologies, site 1 and site 2, the latter both as originally assigned and as reassigned here in light of new results, for the MOFs UiO-66 and NU1000. Note that the originally assigned site 2 was for a $Z r_{6}$ node with the otherwise “missing" proton from site 1 moved to another face of the core (i.e., all of the sites in this scheme have zero net charge and spin).
Carboxylate-containing functional groups or, alternatively, phosphonate groupsl9 have been reported to reactively replace the hydroxyl groups on the $Z r_{6}$ nodes through a solvent-assisted ligand incorporation (SALl) method. We now report how to incorporate methoxy ligands on the node surfaces and through their intermediacy to convert MOF node site 1 into the alternative MOF node site 2. lmportantly, we here reassign the nature of site 2 to involve only a single terminating OH group together with a vacant Zr coordination site. Our results demonstrate a facial ligand exchange on the vacancies of these metal-oxidelike MOF nodes and indicate a subtlety in the acid-base properties of the nodes akin to that noted by Ling and Slater.14
## EXPERIMENTAL AND COMPUTATIONAL METHODS
Synthesis of UiO-66. $Z r C l_{4}$ （ $(99.99\%$ ，0.125 g, $0.54~\mathrm{mmol};$ ）and $\texttt{1m L}$ of concentrated HCl modulator (37 wt%, Sigma-Aldrich) were dissolved in 15 mL of dimethylformamide (DMF， $99.8\%$ Sigma-Aldrich) in an 8-dram vial by using ultrasound for 5 min. The linker precursor benzene1,4-dicarboxylic acid (0.123g, 0.75 mmol, $\geq98.0\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich) was dissolved in the solution with ultrasound applied for about 15 min. The vials were stored at $3531($ for $24\ h$ . The white precipitates that formed were isolated by centrifugation after cooling of the samples to room temperature. The solids were washed with DMF three times to remove unreacted precursors and with acetone six times to remove DMF, and the powder was then dried at room temperature.
Post treatment of UiO-66 in Methanol. UiO66( $20\ m g)$ was placed in $2m L$ of methanol $(\geq99.9\%$ ，Sigma-Aldrich, for HPLC)at $298\textsf{K}$ and held for $48{\textrm{h}}$ . The solution was decanted and replaced three times over the course of the experiment. The powder was dried in air at $298\textsf{K}$ for30 min. The residual methanol was then removed by treatment under dynamic vacuum overnight at $423\ K$
Synthesis of NU-1ooo. A sample consisting of $97\ \mathsf{m g}$ of $Z r0C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O$ 0 $0.30\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ， $98.0\%$ ,SigmaAldrich)and $2700~\mathrm{{mg}}$ 1 $(22\ \mathsf{m m o l}).$ ）of benzoic acid $(\geq99.5\%$ ，Sigma-Aldrich)was mixed with $8~\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF in a 6-dram vial and ultrasonically dissolved. The clear solution was incubated at $353~\mathsf{K}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After cooling of the solution to room temperature, $40\ \mathsf{m g}$ (0.06 mmol) of 1,3,6,8-tetrakis( $p$ -benzoic acid)pyrene $(\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{T B A P y}^{20})$ was added, and the mixture was sonicated for 20 min. The yellow suspension was held at $353~\mathsf{K}$ for $24\ h$ . After cooling of the sample to room temperature, a yellow polycrystalline material was isolated by filtration (35 mg of activated material, $54\%$ yield)and washed with DMF and then activated with HCl. The solid was subsequently washed twice with DMF and six times with acetone. The powder was dried at room temperature.
Post treatment of NU-looo in Methanol. NU1000 $(20{\mathsf{m}}9)$ ）was placed in $2.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol $(\geq99.9\%$ ，Sigma-Aldrich, for HPLC)at $343~\mathsf{K}$ and held for $48{\textrm{h}}$ . The solution was decanted and replaced three times over the course of the experiment. The methanol was then removed by treatment under dynamic vacuum at $298\ K,$ and the sample was activated under dynamic vacuum at $423\mathsf{K}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ .
IR Spectroscopy. A Bruker IFS 66v/S spectrometer with a spectral resolution of $2~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ was used to collect transmission IR spectra of power samples.Approximately $30\ m{\mathfrak{g}}$ of solid sample in an argon-filled glove box was pressed into a thin wafer and loaded into a cell that served as a flow reactor (ln-situ Research lnstitute, South Bend, IN).The cell was sealed and connected to a flow system that allowed recording of spectra while reactant gases flowed through the cell at reaction temperature. Each spectrum is the average of 64 scans.
Isotopic Exchange Experiments. UiO-66 (20 mg)was placed in $2.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{D}}_{4}$ $(99.5\%$ Cambridge lsotope Laboratories) and held at 293 K for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The solution was decanted and replaced three times over the course of the experiment. The methanol- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{D}}_{4}$ was removed by evacuation by treatment under dynamic vacuum overnight at $423\ K$
Models and Computational Details. Cluster Model: NU-looo. Calculations were performed starting from the mix-S topology of the $Z r_{6}$ -NU1000 node, $[\mathsf{Z r}_{6}(\mathsf{\Pi}_{43}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O})_{4}(\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathsf{O H}_{2})_{4}]^{8+}$ , reported by Planas et al.16 The $Z r_{6}$ node of NU-1000 was modeled as a finite cluster extracted from an optimized periodic unit cell to allow a detailed investigation of the plausible new topologies of NU1000. The UiO-66/67 cluster model was obtained from Yang et al.,4 with the organic linkers kept as acetate $(C H_{3}C O_{2}^{-})$ groups as in our previous studies (Figure 1). Moreover, CM5 partial atomic charges2l were computed for site 1, site 2, and other relevant structures (Table S2).
Kohn-Sham Density Functional Calculations. Clusters were optimized using the $M O G-L^{22}$ density functional as implemented in the Gaussian 09 software package,23 together with the def2-TZVP basis set for C,H, O, and Zr atoms. Associated effective core potentials were used on Zr atoms. In all cases, the positions of all atoms were optimized, except for the C and H atoms of the capping acetate groups. The nature of all stationary points was verified by analytical computation of vibrational frequencies, which were also used to compute 298.15 and 343 K thermochemical quantities with all frequencies below $50~\mathsf{c m^{-1}}$ replaced by $50~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ when computing free energies. The SMD continuum solvation model was employed to account for methanol solvation effects when comparing calculated results with those of experiments done in bulk solution,26 except for water and methanol themselves as solutes, for which experimental values/estimates were employed. For details, see the Supporting Information, Sl.
## RESULTS
The IR spectrum of a UiO-66 sample prepared in a synthesis modulated by HCl and washed with acetone is shown in Figure 2. The band at 2746 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ has been assigned to the hydrogen-bonded $0H/O H_{2}$ pairs of site 1 on the $Z r_{6}$ node; the sharp band at $3674\quad{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ ,with two weak shoulders,at 3688 and $3636~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , derives from a combination of the non-hydrogen-bonded OH groups of site 1 together with the (perforce, also non-hydrogenbonded) $\upmu_{3}{\-}0H$ bands, with the majority of the intensity of the $3674~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ band coming from the terminal groups (vide infra).
In the first step to generate site 2 in this work, UiO-66 (modulated by HCl and washed with acetone) was treated in liquid methanol for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ at $298\mathsf{K},$ followed by decanting of the methanol and drying of the sample at $298\mathsf{K}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ ,followed by activation under high vacuum at $423~\mathsf{K}$ for 12 h to remove residual methanol. Analysis indicated that site 1 was completely removed from the nodes after the treatment, as evidenced by the disappearance of the $2746~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ band and a marked decrease in intensity of the $3674~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ band (that component associated with the terminating groups, vide supra), with a concomitant growth of new bands at 2932 and $2828~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure 2). These two new bands are consistent with the C-H stretches of methoxy groups.
It has correspondingly been reported that methanol reacts with the surface hydroxyl groups of bulk zirconia powder to form a methoxy-terminated surface accompanied by the release of water, with the $Z r-O C H_{3}$ species exhibiting IR bands at 2923 and $2817~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . Similarly, methoxy groups on other metal oxides have been characterized by IR bands,at 2960 and $2849~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for $A l_{2}O_{3}$ 30 2920 and $2815~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ 31 and 2958 and $2858~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for $S_{\dot{1}}0_{2}$ .32
We thus infer that methanol reacts similarly with the hydroxyl groups of MOF site 1 of UiO-66 to form Zr-OCH3 at the missing linker sites. To check the assignment, the sample after methanol treatment and activation was immersed in excess methanol- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{D}}_{4}$ at $298\ K$ for $48{\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the ensuing isotopic exchange, the bands at 2932 and 2828 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ had disappeared, with the simultaneous growth of a sharp new band at $2060~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (SI, Figure Sl). The frequency of this band agrees well with that of the IR band at $2062~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ observed for $Z r{-}\mathsf{O C D}_{3}$ on zirconia powder.33
We attempted to carry out the same conversion with UiO-67 but found that this MOF is not stable under the methanol treatment conditions. After 2 h of soaking in methanol, the UiO-67 completely lost its crystallinity.4
To check our ability to identify any physisorbed (as opposed to chemisorbed) methanol on the UiO-66 (and to exclude the possibility of its presence in the aforementioned samples), the methanol-reacted UiO-66 was brought into contact with a pulse of methanol in helium at $373\ K$ 0 $_{\sim100}$ molecules per MOF node; methanol vapor was introduced into the IR cell containing the MOF by bubbling helium carrier gas through liquid methanol). The IR spectrum (Sl, Figure S2) indicated the presence of the expected physisorbed methanol on UiO-66, as judged by new IR bands at 2945 and $2841~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , which match closely those observed for methanol physisorbed on zirconia powder and those observed for methanol vapor.34
To gain further insight into the nature of the methanol substitution process, potential structures and their relative free energies were computed from density functional theory (Mo6-L functional, see the Methods section for computational details). As illustrated in Figure 3, the thermodynamically most favorable structure is 2,which is formed through the substitution of one water molecule by one methanol molecule followed by the loss of the terminating hydroxide as a second water molecule after abstraction of the coordinated methanol proton. (Note that the loss of two equivalents of water becomes still more favorable with methanol containing less than the $1\%$ water that was assumed simply as a convenient standardstate condition.) Structure 2 is predicted to be more stable relative to the adsorption of a second methanol molecule (tautomeric structures 3 and 4), even in liquid methanol.
Computed C-H stretching vibrations for the ${\mathsf{O C H}}_{3}$ group in 2 are predicted, after the application of a scaling factor of 0.958 (a standard value for $M O6-L^{35})$ , to occur at 2913 and $2812c m^{-1}$ . these compare favorably with the experimental values of 2932 and $2828~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (three peaks are computed for the relevant C-H stretches, the symmetric stretch with strong intensity at lower frequency, and two asymmetric stretches, one with strong and one with weak intensity, respectively—experiment does not resolve these two peaks and we take the lowest of the computed values, which provides a peak separation most consistent with the two peaks observed experimentally). With respect to structure 3,which the calculations show to be energetically less favorable, a vibration is predicted at $2618~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for the stretch of the methanol OH group hydrogen bonded to the adjacent methoxide; no such stretch was observed experimentally.
Extending the work from UiO-66 to NU-1000, we conducted similar methanol washing experiments and obtained the IR spectra shown in Figure 4. The sharp band at $2746~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ observed for premethanol-washed NU-looo (spectrum a) is similar to that found for pre-methanol-washed UiO-66 modulated with HCl and washed with acetone, and it is similarly assigned to the hydrogenbonded $0{\mathsf{H}}/{\mathsf{O H}}_{2}$ stretch associated with site 1. We soaked this NU-looo in neat methanol at 343 K for $48{\textrm{h}}$ and then removed the methanol under high vacuum at $298~\mathsf{K}$ followed by further highvacuum treatment at $423\mathsf{K},$ avoiding contact with moisture at all steps. Following these treatments, the band characterizing the hydrogenbonded $0\mathsf{H}/\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ species, at $2746~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ，disappeared, and two new bands appeared, at 2928 and $2825~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (Figure 4, spectrum b), again attributed to methoxy group C-H stretches as observed for UiO-66. Simultaneously, the band at $3674~{\mathsf{c m}}^{\cdot}$ 1, assigned to non-hydrogen-bonded $\upmu_{3}–O H$ and OH and $O H_{2}$ stretches, disappeared, with a new, broader band appearing, centered at $3657~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ which we assign to the $\upmu_{3}–O H$ stretches in the newly formed surface species. Furthermore, a band was observed at $1155~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ , which we associate with the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{v}}_{\mathsf{C O}}$ stretch of the methoxy group. (We note that up to $30\mathrm{-}50\%$ of the methoxy groups adsorbed on bulk zirconia are observed to be coordinated in a $\upmu_{2}$ fashion to two zirconium atoms, as judged by a lower-frequency Vco stretch observed in the spectrum of the bulk. In spectrum b, we observe a very low-intensity peak at 1039 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ that might possibly be attributed to such a species, but DFT does not find such a structure to be stationary, and so we infer that if such species were present, they must been at very low concentration, and that the different local environment of the MOF node compared with a bulk zirconia surface mitigates their formation.)
The same computational procedure described above for UiO-66 was employed to characterize the effect of methanol washing on NU-1ooo, and analogous structure 6 was found again to be the most thermodynamically favored species in contact with methanol solution (Figure 5). For the structure having the topology of 6 on all four NU1000 faces (Figure 5, structure 9), the peaks associated with methoxy group C-H bond stretching are computed to occur at 2927 and $2847~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ again in satisfactory agreement with the experimental values. The computed $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{v}}_{\mathsf{C O}}$ stretch 9 is found at $1129~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (averaged over peaks with strong C-O stretching character separated by 26 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}.$ ), whereas the corresponding peak for the less stable 7 (when considering substitution on a single face) is predicted to be on average at 1049 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ; the former value is in considerably better agreement with experiment than the latter, supporting the inference of the predominant presence of the topology of 6 on each face (i.e., 9) after washing and activation of the MOF.
In additional experiments, we observed that the reaction between methanol and hydroxyl groups on NU-looo nodes also takes place at 298 K,as shown by the results of Figure S3 in Sl, but the conversion under these conditions was incomplete.
We tested the reactivity of node-surface-bound methoxy groups with water, finding that the treatment generated site 2. After treatment of the UiO-66 sample that had been exposed to methanol at $298\mathsf{K}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ and the NU-1000 sample that had been exposed to methanol at $343~\mathsf{K}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ with water vapor at $393\mathsf{K}$ for 30 min (water vapor was introduced into the IR cell containing the MOF by bubbling helium carrier gas through liquid water), the bands in the IR spectrum characterizing the $C-H$ vibrations at 2932 and $2828~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for UiO-66 and the C-H vibrations at 2928 and $2825~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and the C-O vibration at $1155~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for NU-looo decreased substantially (Figure 2c and Figure 4c).
Moreover, a new band was observed at 3781 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , indicating the presence of new terminal OH groups. Methanol produced in the reaction of the methoxy groups with water was observed in the effluent gas by mass spectrometry (Figure S5, Sl). Having calibrated our mass spectrometer with pulses of methanol, we determined how much methanol desorbed as a result of the reaction with water, finding the number of methoxy groups per NU-1000 node to be 3.9. Thus, the number of missing linker sites calculated from the quantified methanol signal (assuming only one methoxy group is bonded to each missing linker site per UiO-66 node), 1.84/12, is in good agreement with the value of 1.75/l2 determined by thermogravimetric analysis of the MOF.36 Consequently,we conclude that structure 2 (and structure 9) is an accurate representation of the structure for UiO-66 (and for NU-l000) after the methanol treatment, respectively.
The methoxy groups are evidently needed as intermediate species to facilitate the conversion from site 1 to site 2 on the node vacancies. When we treated UiO-66 or NU-1000 samples incorporating site 1 with just water in a closed container at $393\ K$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ ，there was no observed conversion of site 1. Nor was site 2 converted to site 1 under such conditions.
The calculated IR spectra of NU-looO structures 6, 9, one monohydroxy-substituted NU1000 site 2, and a NU-looo fully substituted with four sites 2 (designated as 10) are presented in Figure 4. A peak at about $3727~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ was observed for 10, representing the O-H stretching, which is $54~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ blue shifted compared with the experimental value of $3781~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ . The $\upmu_{3}–0–H$ stretching is observed at about $3673\quad{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ for 6, NU-1000 site 2,and 10,whereby the structure 6 band is in good agreement with Figure 4.A.a,which represents the MOF after methanol treatment at 298.15 K. A difference of $22\ c m^{-1}$ was observed for NU-l000 site 2 and 10. For details, see Table S5 in the Sl.
CM5 partial charge analysis was performed for the $z_{\Gamma_{6}}$ nodes having site 1 and site 2 topologies, as well as for structures 2, 6, and 1o (Table S2). The charge increase for the low-coordinate Zr in UiO-66 is $+0.05$ a.u. on going from site 1to structure 2, and $+0.07$ a.u. on going from site 1to site 2. For NU-l0o0, the same change of $+0.07$ a.u. was observed, both on going from site 1 to structure 5,and also at every undercoordinated Zr on going from site 1 to 10. As might be expected, eliminating an aquo ligand increases the electrophilicity of the undercoordinated metal atom in every instance.
To generalize the chemistry, we used ethanol or l-propanol instead of methanol in the reaction with site 1 on UiO-66 nodes, as shown by the results in Figures S6 and S7 in the Sl. The C-H bands for ethoxy groups appear in the respective IR spectra at 2972,2930,and $2871~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ，and these disappeared after contact of the ethoxylated sample with water vapor at $423~\mathsf{K}$ for $\textsf{1h}$ with a simultaneous increase in the $3780~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ band characterizing site 2. The $C-H$ bands for npropoxy groups appeared in the IR spectrum at 2962,2932,2877,and $2859~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ . In contrast to methoxy and ethoxy groups, the propoxy groups were not removed by the water vapor treatment at $423\ K$ for $3h$ ， and we speculate that the lack of reactivity in this instance may be an indication that the alkyl chain of 1-propanol is sufficiently long for favorable van der Waals interactions with the surrounding organic linkers to reduce its effective volatility compared with those of methanol and ethanol.
DISCUSSION
The chemistry of these various face topology changes on the Zr6 nodes is represented in Scheme 2. We emphasize that the methanol (or ethanol) treatment is crucial for the conversion of site 1 to site 2 at moderate temperature, as treatment of either UiO-66 or NU-1000 with water or helium or exposure to high vacuum at $393\ K$ does not lead to loss of the aquo ligand, although treatment at temperatures above $473\ \mathsf{K}$ with the sample under high vacuum does effect conversion (Figure S8, Sl). Considering the reverse reaction,we observed that site 2 is not converted to site 1 by treatment with water at various temperatures, at least over shorter time scales.
That the aquo ligand of site 1 is not lost upon treatment with hot water is perhaps not surprising, particularly given that the gas-phase binding enthalpy of the aquo ligand to Zr in site 1 is predicted to be 34 and 38 kcal/mol in UiO-66 and NU-10oo, respectively. Soaking of the sample in alcohol, however, permits an equilibrium exchange driven by concentration differences (and favorable solvation of lost water) that permits access to the monoalkoxy substituted facial topology (structures 2 and 6 in Figures 3 and 5, respectively), and in contrast to the situation in water, binding of an additional neutral alcohol is not predicted to be favorable. This reasoning sets up a scenario whereby subsequent water treatment reverses the concentration imbalance, and swapping of hydroxide for methoxide is computed to be almost thermoneutral on conversion to a local site 2. What is puzzling, of course, is that treatment of site 2 with high-temperature water does not lead to speedy regeneration of site 1. It may be that the hydrophobic nature of the local environment in contact with the MOF face, which derives from the proximity of the aromatic linkers, leads to a local aqueous structure that is clathrate-like, and that such structuring makes it energetically difficult for a water molecule near the face to recoordinate to Zr. Assessing such a situation would require simulations with explicit solvent (and a suitable model for computing Zr-O interactions) that go beyond the scope of this work, but this is a topic meriting further study. It is known that fully dehydrated NU-looo reverts to fully hydrated NU-looo (i.e., all faces having site 1 topology) when exposed to benchtop humidity for a period of several weeks,37 and so we are confident that the barrier is indeed kinetic in nature.
The chemistry reported here is unique to MOFs because the conversion involves unique terminal OH and/or $O H_{2}$ groups on MOF nodes, and comparable groups are evidently lacking in zeolites and metal oxides. The uniform structures of the MOFs allow unprecedentedly precise control of surfaces with these groups, which are essential for some separations by adsorption,9 for catalysis, and for anchoring of metal complexes.
We note, however, that the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}_{\mathsf{O H}}$ bands of terminal OH groups of site 2 on UiO-66 and NU-1000 have full width at half-maximum (fwhm) values of about 26 and $23\ c m^{-1}$ , respectively. In contrast, the value characterizing the hydroxyl band at $3630~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ in the IR spectrum of SiOHAl groups of HY zeolite (Figure S9 in the Sl) is only $9c m^{-1}$ .A comparison of these values indicates that the degree of uniformity of hydroxyl sites 2 on the $Z r_{6}$ MOF nodes is markedly less than the degree of uniformity of comparable hydroxyl sites on the zeolite, consistent with our previously reported results derived from the fwhm values characterizing $\boldsymbol{v}_{\mathsf{C O}}$ bands of ${\mathsf{I r}}({\mathsf{C O}})_{2}$ species anchored to the MOF nodes and to HY zeolite.4 There is more yet to be learned about the subtle chemistry of these MOF nodes and their surface sites.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, methoxy groups on the $Z r_{6}$ nodes of UiO-66 and NU-1000 (Scheme 1), characterized by IR bands at 2930 and $2828~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and identified by lR spectroscopy and DFT calculations, arise from the reaction of the MOF node hydrogenbonded $0H/O H_{2}$ groups (site 1) with methanol, and the resultant node surface structure is converted into one vacant Zr site and one terminal OH group upon reaction with water. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first example of the tuning of the chemistry of reactive groups (hydroxyl groups) on MOF nodes—and the intermediacy of methoxy or ethoxy groups is crucial for control of this chemistry. Because the DFT calculations demonstrate that the various types of hydroxyl group on the Zr6 nodes have markedly different free energies, and because these groups perform differently from each other in bonding to metal complexes, we may expect the control of these sites to open precise new chemistry, including that of anchored metal complexes, on MOF nodes.
